          %title Ordinal sums
          %h1#ordinal_sums Ordinal sums
          .maruku_toc
            %ul
              %li
                %a{:href => "#idea"} Idea
              %li
                %a{:href => "#recall_finite_ordinals_as_simplicial_sets"} Recall: Finite ordinals as simplicial sets
              %li
                %a{:href => "#the_definition_initially_for_finite_ordinals"} The definition (initially for finite ordinals)
              %ul
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#example"} Example
              %li
                %a{:href => "#ordinal_sum_in_general"} Ordinal sum in general
              %li
                %a{:href => "#ordinal_sum_of_categories"} Ordinal sum of categories
              %li
                %a{:href => "#related_entries"} Related entries
              %li
                %a{:href => "#references"} References
          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            In
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ordinal+arithmetic"} ordinal arithmetic
            %em finite
            ordinals and so provides a useful tool when manipulating
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+sets"} simplicial sets
          %p The basic idea is that in ordinal sum you ‘first put one of the two ordinals and then the other’, so that the elements of the second ordinal are all bigger than those in the first one.
          %p
            Restricted to
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Δ
                    %mi a
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Delta_a
            and thus to finite ordinals, the ordinal sum induces the operation of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/join+of+simplicial+sets"} join of simplicial sets
            endowing the category,
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi sSet
                      %mo +
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} sSet_+
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoidal+category"} monoidal category
          %h2#recall_finite_ordinals_as_simplicial_sets Recall: Finite ordinals as simplicial sets
          %p
            The objects of the
            %em augmented
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplex+category"} simplex category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Δ
                    %mi a
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Delta_a
            can be identified with the finite
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/total+order"} totally ordered
            = succeed "s," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set"} set
            %em including
            the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/empty+set"} empty set
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo ≔
                  %mi ∅
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  [-1] \coloneqq \emptyset
          %p so that then
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mn 0
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo =
                  %mo *
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  [0] = *
          %p is the singleton set, as usual and
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mn 0
                  %mo &lt;
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  [1] = \{ 0 \lt 1\}
          %p
            and so on, so that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mn 0
                  %mo &lt;
                  %mi ⋯
                  %mo &lt;
                  %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [n] = \{ 0 \lt \cdots \lt n\}
          %p
            This counting is off by one compared to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cardinality"} cardinality
            of these sets.
          %p
            (We will restrict for the moment to finite ordinals and thus to the category
            = succeed ".)" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi Δ
                      %mi a
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Delta_a
          %p
            The monoidal structure on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Δ
                    %mi a
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Delta_a
            is, at the level of the sets, just the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/disjoint+union"} disjoint union
          %h2#the_definition_initially_for_finite_ordinals The definition (initially for finite ordinals)
          %p
            If we have
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [n]
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi m
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [m]
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi i
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [n]
            and the other,
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi j
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} j
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi m
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [m]
            we put
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi i
                    %mo &lt;
                    %mi j
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i \lt j
          %h3#example Example
          %p
            As an example, consider
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mn 0
                  %mo &lt;
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [1] = \{ 0 \lt 1\}
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mn 2
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mover
                      %mn 0
                      %mo ¯
                    %mo &lt;
                    %mover
                      %mn 1
                      %mo ¯
                    %mo &lt;
                    %mover
                      %mn 2
                      %mo ¯
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [2] =  \{\overline{0} \lt \overline{1} \lt \overline{2}\}
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo ⊕
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mn 0
                  %mo &lt;
                  %mn 1
                  %mo &lt;
                  %mover
                    %mn 0
                    %mo ¯
                  %mo &lt;
                  %mover
                    %mn 1
                    %mo ¯
                  %mo &lt;
                  %mover
                    %mn 2
                    %mo ¯
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [1] \oplus [2] = \{0 \lt 1 \lt \overline{0} \lt \overline{1} \lt \overline{2}\},
          %p
            which is
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isomorphic"} isomorphic
            as a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/poset"} poset
            to
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mn 4
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [4]
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo ⊕
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mn 3
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [1] \oplus [1] = [3]
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/join+of+simplicial+sets"} join of simplicial sets
            given there.
          %p
            We can thus think of the operation as the
            = succeed "," do
              %strong addition of cardinalities
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [n]
            has
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                  %mo +
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n + 1
            elements. In terms of the counting ‘off-by-one’, this reads
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo ,
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi m
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ↦
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi n
                  %mo +
                  %mi m
                  %mo +
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  ([n], [m]) \mapsto [n + m + 1]
                  \\,,
          %p but must remember there is also the order to keep track of.
          %p
            This operation extends to give the
            %strong ordinal sum
            structure on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Δ
                    %mi a
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Delta_a
            (for details see the discussion in the entry
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplex+category"} simplex category
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoidal+category"} monoidal category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ⊕
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \oplus
            operation described above. Note however that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi m
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo ⊕
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [m]\oplus [n]
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo ⊕
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi m
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [n]\oplus [m]
            %em naturally
            so.
          %h2#ordinal_sum_in_general Ordinal sum in general
          %p
            If we consider ordinals that may not be finite then clearly the idea of ordinal sum generalises, so, for ordinals,
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi α
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi β
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \beta
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi α
                  %mo ⊕
                  %mi β
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha\oplus \beta
            is the set
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi α
                  %mo ⊔
                  %mi β
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha\sqcup \beta
            with total order in which elements in either part are ordered as originally, but where any
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi α
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a\in \alpha
            is less than and
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi b
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi β
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b\in \beta
          %p
            If either ordinal is infinite, this may lead to
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi α
                  %mo ⊕
                  %mi β
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha\oplus \beta
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi β
                  %mo ⊕
                  %mi α
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \beta\oplus \alpha
            not being isomorphic. For instance,
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mn 0
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo ⊕
                  %mi ω
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [0]\oplus\omega
            is isomorphic to
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ω
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \omega
            itself, but
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ω
                  %mo ⊕
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mn 0
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \omega\oplus [0]
            has a greatest element, i.e. the
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0
            in
            = succeed "-summand," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mn 0
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [0]
          %p
            We thus give a
            = succeed ":" do
              %em warning
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoidal+structure"} monoidal structure
            given by ordinal sum is not a symmetric monoidal category structure even when restricted to the subcategory of finite ordinals. If one extends to consider infinite ordinals, there may even be no order preserving maps at all from
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi i
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo ⊕
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi j
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [i] \oplus [j]
            to
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi j
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo ⊕
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi i
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [j] \oplus [i]
            for
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi i
                    %mo ≠
                    %mi j
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i \neq j
          %h2#ordinal_sum_of_categories Ordinal sum of categories
          %p
            In “Ordinal Sums and Equational Doctrines”, Lawvere defines the
            %strong
              ordinal sum
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %msub
                      %mo +
                      %mi σ
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A +_\sigma B
              of two categories
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
            as the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pushout"} pushout
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi A
                          %mo ×
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                          %mn 2
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                          %mo ×
                          %mi B
                        %mtd
                          %mo →
                        %mtd
                          %mi A
                          %mo ×
                          %mn 2
                          %mo ×
                          %mi B
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi A
                          %mo +
                          %mi B
                        %mtd
                          %mo →
                        %mtd
                          %mi A
                          %msub
                            %mo +
                            %mi σ
                          %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{
                  A \times |2| \times B &amp;\to&amp; A \times 2 \times B \\
                  \\downarrow &amp; &amp; \downarrow \\
                  A + B &amp;\to &amp; A +_\sigma B
                  }
          %p
            where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mo +
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A+B
            is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coproduct"} coproduct
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/interval+category"} interval category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} |2|
            is its underlying discrete category of objects, and the left vertical arrow is defined by
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                  %mi a
                  %mo ,
                  %mi i
                  %mo ,
                  %mi b
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                  %mo =
                  %mrow
                    %mo {
                    %mrow
                      %mtable{:columnalign => "left left", :displaystyle => "false"}
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi a
                          %mtd
                            %mi i
                            %mo =
                            %mn 0
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi b
                          %mtd
                            %mi i
                            %mo =
                            %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\lang a,i,b\rang = \begin{cases}a &amp; i = 0 \\ b &amp; i = 1\end{cases}
          %p
            Concretely,
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %msub
                    %mo +
                    %mi σ
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A +_\sigma B
            may be described as the category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mo +
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A + B
            together with exactly one arrow
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                  %mo →
                  %mi b
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a \to b
            adjoined for every object
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi A
                  %mo ,
                  %mi b
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a \in A, b \in B
            (in other words, as the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cograph+of+a+profunctor"} collage
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
            along the terminal profunctor).
          %p
            Equivalently,
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %msub
                    %mo +
                    %mi σ
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A +_\sigma B
            may be described as the colimit of the diagram
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mi A
                          %mo ×
                          %mi B
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mi A
                          %mo ×
                          %mi B
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mpadded{:lspace => "-100%width", :width => "0"}
                            %mrow
                              %msub
                                %mi π
                                %mn 1
                          %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                          %mo ↙
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo ↘
                          %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                          %mpadded{:width => "0"}
                            %mrow
                              %msub
                                %mi i
                                %mn 0
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mpadded{:lspace => "-100%width", :width => "0"}
                            %mrow
                              %msub
                                %mi i
                                %mn 1
                          %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                          %mo ↙
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo ↘
                          %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                          %mpadded{:width => "0"}
                            %mrow
                              %msub
                                %mi π
                                %mn 2
                        %mtd
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi A
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mi A
                          %mo ×
                          %mn 2
                          %mo ×
                          %mi B
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mi B
                          %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{
                  \&amp; &amp; A \times B &amp; &amp; &amp; &amp; A \times B &amp; &amp; \\
                  \&amp; \mathllap{\pi_1}\; \swarrow &amp; &amp; \searrow \; \mathrlap{i_0} &amp; &amp; \mathllap{i_1}\; \swarrow &amp; &amp; \searrow \; \mathrlap{\pi_2} &amp; \\
                  A &amp; &amp; &amp; &amp; A \times 2 \times B &amp; &amp; &amp; &amp; B.
                  }
          %h2#related_entries Related entries
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/join+of+categories"} join of categories
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/join+of+simplicial+sets"} join of simplicial sets
          %h2#references References
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/William+Lawvere"}> William Lawvere
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Ordinal sums and equational doctrines
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Seminar+on+Triples+and+Categorical+Homology+Theory"} Seminar on Triples and Categorical Homology Theory
              LNM
              %strong 80
              (1969) 141-155 &amp;lbrack;
              %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1007/BFb0083085"}> doi:10.1007/BFb0083085
              \&amp;rbrack;
        .revisedby
          %p
